#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80864d0c-9c25-5917-9cd1-b834c6eeb9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124031} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffb7ba6-d93b-5634-94dc-f36b69e7110d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124032} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d25bf8-f594-5faa-9016-1c42f19cf2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124033} {:answer/id #uuid "d0aa8288-d4be-5cc6-b9ce-b300b76c0245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124034} {:answer/id #uuid "d891e277-b70b-55aa-a6e6-c0c462d0e220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d891e277-b70b-55aa-a6e6-c0c462d0e220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124035} :answer-field/id #uuid "39961aff-5f21-5847-aed7-a90bc54161f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" :question/math-academy-id "q-97051" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- $A+2I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2daa22-1727-5a47-850e-1f9e36cb83d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384619} {:answer/id #uuid "650d3df9-0dfc-5d9d-95d6-53e957a7bad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384620} {:answer/id #uuid "f27da12d-2633-5cc5-ad9f-cb1d359e064c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384621} {:answer/id #uuid "06ef2ccc-b41d-5292-8290-0acb5f6567e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384622} {:answer/id #uuid "79294574-7441-5453-865a-e124567cf0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79294574-7441-5453-865a-e124567cf0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384623} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c78716-464b-5ab8-bd64-0dcbe92d55db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bae13f0-ed61-5405-af6c-4709e99174f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97897" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79ba487f-28c5-5d1b-9e56-d271d9ce9e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384626} {:answer/id #uuid "add9c991-f8b2-51b5-895e-990ca0db9f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384627} {:answer/id #uuid "5a84ce37-8f0a-5827-939f-9a4330caa90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384628} {:answer/id #uuid "181ff9b1-dc77-57c8-8058-5983ffd433d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384629} {:answer/id #uuid "720e8604-6c59-5755-887f-9bc351c6a1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a84ce37-8f0a-5827-939f-9a4330caa90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384628} :answer-field/id #uuid "6975df52-b21a-5a04-958e-e8353d9cf26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aa9b75a-073a-5562-97b5-f48211bd8152" :question/math-academy-id "q-97948" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C-5I$ is a non-singular matrix.\n- $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3$\n- $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-5I)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c16846-45cc-58cb-8a74-6f414a3f7aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124067} {:answer/id #uuid "0998daf3-15d4-557f-a070-5b9d4c3bd56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124068} {:answer/id #uuid "2251e4e2-32fd-5c6d-8e5d-8ab934d1aa31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124069} {:answer/id #uuid "37577466-b052-52e5-9ec2-dcf7a46a2d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124070} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5de913-7853-54fa-a759-ff669ebf52c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d5de913-7853-54fa-a759-ff669ebf52c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124071} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e09feb7-9b1d-5d3b-abd6-573094ba53f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b68a0678-9348-57e6-b4b3-d9e83bab09ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-97961" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd5bb9d6-bfd5-58a8-8976-f6f11a7fad53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384633} {:answer/id #uuid "71a86466-ffa3-5b77-9b27-b82a6408f55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384634} {:answer/id #uuid "d7919dd8-ed38-52f0-a754-938b9dcd75ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384635} {:answer/id #uuid "1820f85e-ec5f-5411-8cd0-ce8cffc2d20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384636} {:answer/id #uuid "f786244b-d7da-5cd5-a693-cd6a8506d190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1820f85e-ec5f-5411-8cd0-ce8cffc2d20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384636} :answer-field/id #uuid "0885b831-dde7-5596-ad72-d6e1b784a642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c08b449a-000e-584f-a553-bf350d70d374" :question/math-academy-id "q-97969" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n- $A-3I$ is invertible\n- $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
